<spell-notebook "card usage">
	<header level="1">
		Card
		<header>A card displays site content in a manner similar to a playing card.</>
	</>
	<card-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#groups">Groups</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			A card is an `<article>`: a self-contained piece of the page, such as a person, a product or a post.
			With `link="..."` the whole card becomes ONE link (an `<a>`), named by its text. `appearance="link"` is only the
			hover lift, so a card that goes somewhere needs `link`.

			- Its content is the generic [content parts](/components/ui/ui-header.html): `<ui-content>`, `<ui-header>`,
			  `<ui-meta>`, `<ui-description>`, `<ui-extra>` and `<ui-avatar>` inside a card take the card's
			  look, so there is no `ui-card-header`.
			- A slotted `<img>` is a full-width image (a `<ui-image>` isn't: slot a plain `<img>`, or use
			  `image`).
			- The shorthand attributes `image`, `header`, `meta`, `description` and `extra` render the same
			  parts for you, in that order: the image, one content block, your own content, then the extra
			  content. Slot a part of the same kind (your own `<ui-header>`, or an `<img>` for `image`) and
			  that shorthand steps aside.
			- Cards fire no events of their own.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-card link="/people/kristy">
			  <img src="kristy.png" alt="Kristy" />
			  <ui-content>
			    <ui-header>Kristy</ui-header>
			    <ui-meta>Joined in 2013</ui-meta>
			    <ui-description>Kristy is an art director living in New York.</ui-description>
			  </ui-content>
			  <ui-extra>22 Friends</ui-extra>
			</ui-card>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Groups">
		<markdown>
			A `<ui-cards>` group hands its variations to its cards: `size`, `color`, `direction`, `appearance`
			and `inverted` on the group apply to every card that doesn't set its own (a card's looks join the group's).

			- `columns` sets how many cards share a row; without it each card keeps a fixed width and the row
			  wraps.
			- `doubling` and `stackable` follow the group's own width, not the window's: a group narrower than
			  768px shows fewer cards to a row (`doubling`) or one (`stackable`), so a group in a narrow column
			  stacks even on a wide screen.
			- Cards in a group match heights row by row.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			A card takes no focus of its own; a link card (`link`) is one Tab stop, and **Enter** follows it.
			Links, buttons and inputs inside a plain card are Tab stops as usual.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A card is an `<article>`, so a screen reader can move from card to card. Give its `<ui-header>`
			  a `level` when the card's title should also be a heading in the page outline.
			- A link card is one link whose name is all of its text. Keep other links and buttons out of it:
			  nested interactive content can't be reached or named properly. A card with its own links (a
			  linked header, an extra link) should not have `link`.
			- `disabled` sets `aria-disabled`, and a link card loses its `link`, so it is no longer a Tab stop.
			- `loading` sets `aria-busy` and announces "Loading…" (translatable) through a hidden status
			  message.
			- A `<ui-cards>` group is a `role="list"` and each card in it a `listitem`, so a reader hears how
			  many cards there are.
			- The `image` shorthand is decorative (`alt=""`) unless you give `alt`; a slotted `<img>` needs its
			  own `alt`.
			- Colour is never the only signal: a coloured card's meaning belongs in its text.
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its slotted parts through a bare `<slot>`, so their text,
			  images and links stay; the card's own box and link, the shorthands, the group's variations,
			  `aria-busy` and the loading announcement go.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>
			A card has no events or rich properties: write it like any element, with the shorthands bound to
			your data.
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-cards columns="3">
			  {people.map((p) => <ui-card key={p.id} link={p.url} image={p.photo} alt={p.name} heading={p.name} meta={p.role} />)}
			</ui-cards>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-cards columns="3">
			  <ui-card v-for="p in people" :key="p.id" :href="p.url" :image="p.photo" :alt="p.name" :heading="p.name" />
			</ui-cards>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-cards columns="3">
			  <For each={people()}>{(p) => <ui-card link={p.url} image={p.photo} alt={p.name} heading={p.name} />}</For>
			</ui-cards>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
